## Definition and domains

For real b>0, lg b=log_10 b is the unique real exponent t with 10^t=b. Base 10 is positive, not 1, and t->10^t is strictly increasing from R onto (0,infinity). Hence lg b=t iff 10^t=b; 10^(lg b)=b only for b>0; lg(10^t)=t for all real t.
The examples give lg1000=3, lg35 is the unique exponent giving 35, 10^(lg100)=100, 10^(lg0.1)=0.1 and 10^(lg5)=5.
The source footnote makes unqualified "a number's logarithm" mean its common logarithm. Do not remove it or substitute a new base.
For each log expression below, first require its full original argument to be real and strictly positive. Cancellations never restore undefined points. Finite constants have no free-variable domain; their argument positivity is checked individually.
All variable domains below are subsets of real numbers. Principal even roots are nonnegative; all constant radicals here are positive.

